<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.1" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="42349-1" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Reason for referral (narrative)"/>
    <title>Reason for Referral</title>
    <text><paragraph>Moderate Alzheimer&apos;s disease in a 90-year-old man one month after a fall at home and repair of a right hip fracture. Please confirm the stage, review whether donepezil is still worthwhile given his heart rate and fall risk, and advise on mild evening restlessness without antipsychotics. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Urgency: Routine, within two weeks, on the same day as his two-week primary care check. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Questions for the consultant:</paragraph><list listType="ordered"><item>Do you agree that his dementia is at the moderate stage (FAST stage 5)?</item><item>Is donepezil still giving benefit, and is it safe to continue with a heart rate in the low 60s and a recent fall?</item><item>How should the evening restlessness and repeated questions be managed without antipsychotics or sedatives?</item><item>Would you add memantine now, and is any further testing needed?</item></list></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Mr. Reagan, a 90-year-old man with Alzheimer&apos;s disease diagnosed many years ago, is seen at the request of Dr. Ellison six weeks after a fall at home and internal fixation of a right hip fracture. He takes part in the history as he is able, and his caregivers add detail. Since the hospital oxycodone and diphenhydramine were stopped two weeks ago he sleeps as well as before and has been no more confused. In the evenings he is mildly restless and asks the same questions again; a quiet, unhurried evening routine settles him. He has had no hallucinations, aggression, low mood, fainting, or lightheadedness on standing, and no nausea on donepezil, which he takes each morning. He walks short distances in the house with a front-wheeled walker and standby help and has had no further falls. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Historical basis. These claims are historical evidence, not findings from a modern examination.</paragraph><list><item>Ronald Reagan was born on February 6, 1911, in Tampico, Illinois, to Jack and Nelle Reagan; his older brother Neil was born in 1908. [D]</item><item>In a letter to the American people made public on November 5, 1994, Reagan wrote that he had recently been told he was one of the millions of Americans who would be afflicted with Alzheimer&apos;s disease, and that as the disease progresses the family often bears a heavy burden. [D]</item><item>Reagan&apos;s personal physicians stated in November 1994 that his annual examinations included memory testing, that over the preceding twelve months they had seen findings suggesting possible early Alzheimer&apos;s disease, and that after further testing and observation they concluded he was entering its early stages. [D]</item><item>Reagan was thrown from a horse in Mexico on July 4, 1989; at a routine examination at the Mayo Clinic in September 1989, doctors found fluid on his brain attributed to the fall, and it was removed surgically at St. Mary&apos;s Hospital in Rochester, Minnesota. [D]</item><item>Reagan fell at home on January 12, 2001, and broke his right hip; the next day, at St. John&apos;s Health Center in Santa Monica, surgeons repaired it with a pin, plate, and screws in a 65-minute operation. He went home on January 20, 2001, and continued physical therapy at home, with no weight-bearing sessions for at least two weeks. [D]</item><item>Reagan survived an assassination attempt in 1981, had an operation for colon cancer in 1985, and had prostate and skin-cancer surgery in 1987. [D]</item><item>Reagan&apos;s father, Jack, died of a heart attack on May 18, 1941, and his brother, Neil, died of heart failure on December 11, 1996. [D]</item><item>Reagan&apos;s mother, Nelle, died on July 25, 1962, of complications of Alzheimer&apos;s disease, according to the Reagan Library. [D]</item></list><paragraph>1 later historical claim is omitted because it describes events after the index age. See the teaching note.</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

